WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.190
STEPHANIE WONG: As your
cloud application scales,

00:00:02.190 --> 00:00:05.460
you'll eventually be faced
with an all too common problem.

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:08.847
How do I maintain tight
control over network resources,

00:00:08.847 --> 00:00:11.430
but not be a roadblock to teams
spinning up the resources they

00:00:11.430 --> 00:00:12.720
need to do work?

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:14.250
In this episode of
Cloud Networking,

00:00:14.250 --> 00:00:16.830
I show you that you can have
the best of both worlds using

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:17.690
shared VPCs.

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:25.180
Large organizations with
multiple cloud projects

00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:27.010
value the ability
to share resources

00:00:27.010 --> 00:00:29.680
while maintaining logical
separation between groups

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:30.890
or departments.

00:00:30.890 --> 00:00:32.980
And this makes complete sense.

00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:34.900
Centrally network
admins are on the hook

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:37.510
to maintain sanity for
the overall network,

00:00:37.510 --> 00:00:40.400
so sharing resources is
critically important.

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:42.760
But every team
has special needs,

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:45.040
meaning that it's a
constant churn for admins

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:48.280
to have to set up new projects,
access policies, billing,

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:50.710
quotas, et cetera, et cetera.

00:00:50.710 --> 00:00:54.380
If your company is large enough,
this can be a full time job.

00:00:54.380 --> 00:00:57.040
That's why Google
created shared VPC, which

00:00:57.040 --> 00:00:59.200
allows an organization
to connect resources

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:02.230
from multiple projects
to a common VPC network,

00:01:02.230 --> 00:01:04.750
so that they can communicate
with each other securely

00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:08.350
and efficiently using internal
IPs from that network.

00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:11.320
When you use shared VPC,
you designate a project

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:15.640
as a host project and attach one
or more service projects to it.

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:17.800
The VPC networks in
the host projects

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:20.170
are called shared VPC networks.

00:01:20.170 --> 00:01:22.210
With shared VPC,
you can centrally

00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:25.990
manage the creation of routes,
firewalls, subnet IP ranges,

00:01:25.990 --> 00:01:29.470
VPN connections, and more
for the entire organization,

00:01:29.470 --> 00:01:32.680
and allow developers to own
billing, quotas, and IAM

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:35.050
permissions, and
autonomously operate

00:01:35.050 --> 00:01:36.730
their development projects.

00:01:36.730 --> 00:01:38.710
For example, imagine
you were the network

00:01:38.710 --> 00:01:40.810
admin at an e-commerce company.

00:01:40.810 --> 00:01:43.450
You have a single externally
facing website application

00:01:43.450 --> 00:01:46.360
server that uses services
like personalization,

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:50.020
recommendation, and analytics
all internally available,

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:52.810
but built by different
development teams.

00:01:52.810 --> 00:01:54.490
You can have a
shared VPC network

00:01:54.490 --> 00:01:56.290
with a host project
and three service

00:01:56.290 --> 00:01:59.920
projects for personalization,
recommendation, and analytics

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:01.960
all on different subnets.

00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:04.498
With shared VPC, the
network and security admin

00:02:04.498 --> 00:02:06.790
would set up the overall
security policies and the host

00:02:06.790 --> 00:02:09.880
project, like restricting
which VMs can have public IPs

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:11.980
and access to the
internet by setting up

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:14.410
an organization
policy that disables

00:02:14.410 --> 00:02:16.960
external IP access for VMs.

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:19.720
In this example, the
personalization recommendation

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:21.418
and analytic services.

00:02:21.418 --> 00:02:23.710
Meanwhile, each team would
be able to start spinning up

00:02:23.710 --> 00:02:25.540
VMs in their assigned
service project

00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:27.190
and decide more fine
grained details,

00:02:27.190 --> 00:02:29.560
like setting up
compute resources.

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:33.100
And VMs still get the same
network throughput caps and VM

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:36.670
to VM latency as when they're
not on shared networks.

00:02:36.670 --> 00:02:40.330
Let's dive into how to
set up a shared VPC.

00:02:40.330 --> 00:02:44.260
At the organizational level,
my log in, Stephanie.Wong,

00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:48.560
currently has org admin and
shared VPC admin privileges.

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:50.800
I can also assign the
shared VPC admin role

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:52.750
to somebody else in my org.

00:02:52.750 --> 00:02:54.430
Switching over to
my host project,

00:02:54.430 --> 00:02:58.630
I created a custom VPC called
VPC one and two subnets,

00:02:58.630 --> 00:03:00.850
development and production.

00:03:00.850 --> 00:03:03.070
I also created two
firewall rules,

00:03:03.070 --> 00:03:05.170
one to allow SSH
to all instances

00:03:05.170 --> 00:03:08.890
in the network and one to allow
ICMP traffic between instances

00:03:08.890 --> 00:03:10.460
in these subnets.

00:03:10.460 --> 00:03:13.630
In the shared VPC page,
first, enable host project

00:03:13.630 --> 00:03:17.860
to be the host project by
clicking set up shared VPC.

00:03:17.860 --> 00:03:20.620
I'm going to select
sharing individual subnets.

00:03:20.620 --> 00:03:23.920
This option allows you to share
specific subnets in the host

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:26.920
project VPC with
your service project.

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:31.090
Then select the VPC one
dev and prod subnets.

00:03:31.090 --> 00:03:33.310
Let's attach two service
projects, development

00:03:33.310 --> 00:03:34.610
and production.

00:03:34.610 --> 00:03:36.580
Make sure the Compute
Engine API has

00:03:36.580 --> 00:03:40.270
been enabled in each service
project before attaching them.

00:03:40.270 --> 00:03:42.160
Here, you can edit the
default permissions

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:44.550
given to this service project.

00:03:44.550 --> 00:03:46.640
Then click Save.

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:49.040
You can now see host
project is the shared VPC

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:51.380
host with two
subnets, and it has

00:03:51.380 --> 00:03:53.420
two attached service projects.

00:03:53.420 --> 00:03:56.060
This is also where you can add
additional host project users

00:03:56.060 --> 00:03:57.950
and admins.

00:03:57.950 --> 00:04:00.350
Now I'm going to log into
my development project

00:04:00.350 --> 00:04:05.720
on our account, John, who only
has permission to that project.

00:04:05.720 --> 00:04:16.149
Switching to the development
project, create a new VM,

00:04:16.149 --> 00:04:19.550
and add a development tag.

00:04:19.550 --> 00:04:23.510
Then select network shared
with me from the host projects,

00:04:23.510 --> 00:04:26.860
so that you can
select the dev subnet.

00:04:26.860 --> 00:04:29.890
Once it's done creating, copy
the internal IP of the instance

00:04:29.890 --> 00:04:33.330
since you'll need
it just in a second.

00:04:33.330 --> 00:04:34.910
Next, I'll switch
to Mary's account,

00:04:34.910 --> 00:04:38.000
who is the owner of
the production project.

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:40.070
Create a new VM in
the production subnet

00:04:40.070 --> 00:04:41.722
and add a production tag.

00:04:41.722 --> 00:04:44.180
Making sure to change the zone
to the one in the production

00:04:44.180 --> 00:04:46.190
subnet region, select
the prod subnet.

00:04:55.800 --> 00:04:58.140
When both VMs are done
creating, you can see.

00:04:58.140 --> 00:05:01.740
I can SSH into the prod instance
and ping the dev instances

00:05:01.740 --> 00:05:04.980
internal IP address since I've
created the right firewall

00:05:04.980 --> 00:05:08.850
rules and the host
project shared VPC.

00:05:08.850 --> 00:05:11.490
Now you just want to make sure
that when configuring subnet IP

00:05:11.490 --> 00:05:13.590
ranges in the same
or different regions,

00:05:13.590 --> 00:05:16.440
you allow for sufficient IP
space between the subnets

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:17.790
for future growth.

00:05:17.790 --> 00:05:20.820
Plus GCP lets you expand
an existing subnet

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:23.890
without affecting any
existing VM's IP address,

00:05:23.890 --> 00:05:25.470
and with zero downtime.

00:05:25.470 --> 00:05:26.620
How cool is that?

00:05:26.620 --> 00:05:28.890
Shared VPC is a
powerful feature that

00:05:28.890 --> 00:05:31.050
makes GCP more
flexible and manageable

00:05:31.050 --> 00:05:32.812
for your organization.

00:05:32.812 --> 00:05:34.770
Yep, that means you might
just have enough time

00:05:34.770 --> 00:05:36.850
to go fishing on the
weekend without a fire drill

00:05:36.850 --> 00:05:39.900
ping interrupting
that overhead cast.

00:05:39.900 --> 00:05:42.880
To learn more, check out the
documentation link below.

00:05:42.880 --> 00:05:44.520
And remember,
optimizing your network

00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:46.860
means freeing up your bandwidth.

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:49.610
[MUSIC PLAYING]

